WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:02.890
Hello and welcome back.

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:08.810
In this week, we're going to go over the basics of neural network programming.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:13.630
It turns out that when you implement a neural network, there are some implementation techniques

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:16.250
that are going to be really important.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:21.850
For example, if you have a training set of M training examples, you might be used to

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:28.210
processing the training set by having a for loop step through your M training examples.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:32.930
But it turns out that when you implement a neural network, you usually want to process

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:38.610
your entire training set without using an explicit for loop to loop over your entire training

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:39.610
set.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.130
So you see how to do that in this week's materials.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:48.810
Another idea, when you organize the computation of a neural network, usually you have what's

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:53.490
called a forward pass or forward propagation step followed by a backward pass or what's

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.130
called a backward propagation step.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:01:01.130
And so in this week's materials, you also get an intuition about why the computations

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:07.250
in learning in a neural network can be organized in this board propagation and a separate backward

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:09.170
propagation.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:14.010
For this week's materials, I want to convey these ideas using logistic regression in order

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.230
to make the ideas easier to understand.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:20.410
But even if you've seen logistic regression before, I think that there'll be some new

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:23.850
and interesting ideas for you to pick up in this week's materials.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.850
So with that, let's get started.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:30.650
Logistic regression is an algorithm for binary classification.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:33.250
So let's start by setting up the problem.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:36.950
Here's an example of a binary classification problem.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:44.470
You might have an input of an image like that and one to output a label to recognize

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:50.410
this image as being either a cat, in which case you output one or non-cat, in which

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.370
case you output zero.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:57.870
And we're going to use Y to denote the output label.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.530
Let's look at how an image is represented in a computer.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:07.090
To store an image, your computer stores three separate matrices corresponding to the red,

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:11.070
green and blue color channels of this image.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:20.570
So if your input image is 64 pixels by 64 pixels, then you would have three 64 by 64

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:27.050
matrices corresponding to the red, green and blue pixel intensity values for your image.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:31.050
Although to make these from a slide, I drew these as much smaller matrices.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:35.370
So these are actually 5 by 4 matrices rather than 64 by 64.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:41.930
So to turn these pixel intensity values into a feature vector, what we're going to do

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:47.810
is unroll all of these pixel values into a input feature vector X.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:55.170
So to unroll all of these pixel intensity values into a feature vector, what we're going to do

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:59.810
is define a feature vector X corresponding to this image as follows.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:08.770
We're just going to take all the pixel values, 255, 231 and so on, 255, 231 and so on, until

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:16.330
we've listed all the red pixels and then eventually 255, 134, 255, 134 and so on,

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:23.490
until we get a very long feature vector listing out all the red, green and blue pixel intensity

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.770
values of this image.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:35.330
So if this image is a 64 by 64 image, the total dimension of this vector X will be 64

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:41.810
by 64 by 3 because that's the total numbers we have in all of these matrices, which in

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:45.050
this case turns out to be 1, 2, 2, 8, 8.

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:47.290
That's what you get if you multiply out those numbers.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:53.690
And so we're going to use nX equals 1, 2, 2, 8, 8 to represent the dimension of the input

00:03:53.690 --> 00:04:00.290
features X. And sometimes for brevity, I might also just use lowercase n to represent the

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:02.770
dimension of this input feature vector.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:07.890
So in binary classification, our goal is to learn a classifier that can input an image

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:15.730
represented by its feature vector X and predict whether the corresponding label Y is 1 or 0,

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.090
that is whether this is a cat image or a non-cat image.

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:23.650
Let's now lay out some of the notation that we'll use throughout the rest of this course.

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:34.410
A single training example is represented by a pair X comma Y, where X is an nX dimensional

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:39.650
feature vector and Y, the label, is either 0 or 1.

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:45.050
Your training sets will comprise lowercase m training examples.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:51.090
And so your training sets will be written X1 comma Y1, which is the input and output

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:55.130
for your first training example, X2 comma Y2.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:05:02.010
For your second training example up to Xm comma Ym, which is your last training example,

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:05.530
then that altogether is your entire training set.

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:10.010
So I'm going to use lowercase m to denote the number of training examples.

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:14.210
And sometimes to emphasize that this is the number of training examples, I might write

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.010
this as m subscript train.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:21.770
And when we talk about the test set, we might sometimes use m subscript test to denote

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.290
the number of test examples.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:27.250
So that's the number of test examples.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:33.650
Finally, to put all of the training examples into a more compact notation, we're going

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:42.010
to define a matrix capital X as defined by taking your training set inputs, X1, X2,

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:44.850
and so on, and stacking them in columns.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:52.010
So if you take X1 and put that as the first column of this matrix, X2, put that as the

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:58.370
second column, and so on, down to Xm, then this is the matrix capital X.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:05.890
So this matrix X will have m columns, where m is the number of training examples, and

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:09.530
the number of rows, or the height of this matrix, is nx.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:16.050
Notice that in other clauses, you might see the matrix capital X defined by stacking up

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:23.930
the training examples in rows, like so, X1 transpose down to Xm transpose.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:28.650
But it turns out that when you're implementing neural networks, using this convention I have

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:32.010
on the left will make the implementation much easier.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:39.130
So just to recap, X is a nx by m dimensional matrix.

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:45.170
And when you implement this in Python, you see that X dot shape, that's the Python command

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:50.610
for finding the shape of a matrix, that this is an nx, m.

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:53.810
This just means it's an nx by m dimensional matrix.

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:58.770
So that's how you group the training examples input X into a matrix.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:01.290
How about the output labels Y?

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:06.330
It turns out that to make your implementation of a neural network easier, it will be convenient

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:10.050
to also stack Y in columns.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:18.570
So we're going to define capital Y to be equal to Y1, Y2, up to Ym, like so.

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:25.130
So Y here will be a 1 by m dimensional matrix.

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:32.010
And again, to use the Python notation, the shape of Y will be 1, m, which just means

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:34.890
this is a 1 by m matrix.

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:39.490
And as you implement your neural network later in this course, you find that a useful

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:44.890
convention will be to take the data associated with different training examples, and by data

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:50.490
I mean either X or Y or other quantities you see later, but to take the stuff or the data

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:55.370
associated with different training examples and to stack them in different columns, like

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:58.450
we've done here for both X and Y.

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:02.490
So that's the notation we'll use for logistic regression and for neural networks

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:04.070
later in this course.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:08.430
If you ever forget what a piece of notation means, like what is m or what is n or what

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:12.550
is something else, we've also posted on the course website a notation guide that you

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:17.430
can use to quickly look up what any particular piece of notation means.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:21.190
So with that, let's go on to the next video where we'll start to flesh out logistic

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:23.150
regression using this notation.
